1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,270
Time flies, or so they say.
人们常说，时光飞逝。

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:09,940
No matter where you are, humans are constantly measuring and checking time.
无论你身在何处，人类不断地计量和查看时间。

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:22,890
Some of us are good at it – planning and doing things way ahead of time - while others are always trying to beat the clock and do things at the eleventh hour.
我们中的一些人很擅长这一点——提前规划和做事——而另一些人总是试图赶时间，在最后一刻才做事。

4
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:25,540
And that’s only if you’re on time.
而这还只是在你准时的情况下。

5
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:29,500
What about if you are behind time?
如果你迟到了呢？

6
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,550
Tardiness can be serious.
迟到可能是很严重的问题。

7
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:36,200
If you were one minute late for work, would you resign?
如果你上班迟到了一分钟，你会辞职吗？

8
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:48,830
It may sound extreme to many of us, but that is exactly what occurred in the UK upper chamber of parliament, the House of Lords, in January 2018.
这对我们很多人来说可能听起来很极端，但这正是2018年1月在英国议会上议院发生的事情。

9
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:59,660
International development minister Lord Bates arrived one minute late, and, as a result, was unable to answer a scheduled question.
国际发展部大臣贝茨勋爵（Lord Bates）迟到了一分钟，结果无法回答一个预定的问题。

10
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:07,270
Instead of trying to make up for lost time, as many might do, he resigned on the spot.
他没有像很多人可能会做的那样试图抓紧时间，而是当场辞职。

11
00:01:07,270 --> 00:01:12,060
He apologised for his discourtesy and stated that he was ashamed.
他为自己的失礼道歉，并表示自己很羞愧。

12
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:18,420
His resignation, though, was not accepted by the UK prime minister.
不过，英国首相没有接受他的辞职。

13
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:22,020
So, how late is too late?
那么，迟到多晚算非常晚呢？

14
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:32,760
Many cultures take punctuality very seriously, whereas others seem to accept lagging behind as just the normal way of things.
许多文化非常重视守时，而其他文化似乎把落后（迟到）当作正常的事情来接受。

15
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:44,550
Members of the BBC from various different cultures were asked about the concept of timekeeping in their native countries and responded with a raft of answers.
来自不同文化的英国广播公司（BBC）员工被问及他们本国的守时观念，他们给出了许多答案。

16
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:55,220
BBC employees from Latin America, Rwanda and Sri Lanka said that there are more flexible attitudes to timekeeping in their cultures.
来自拉丁美洲、卢旺达和斯里兰卡的BBC员工表示，在他们的文化中，人们对守时的态度更加灵活。

17
00:01:55,220 --> 00:02:04,670
In Latin America, things may happen five minutes, 20 minutes, an hour or even two after they were planned.
在拉丁美洲，事情可能会在计划时间之后的五分钟、二十分钟、一个小时甚至两个小时才发生。

18
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:10,070
Whereas, in Sri Lanka, lateness is a part of the daily routine.
而在斯里兰卡，迟到是日常生活的一部分。

19
00:02:10,070 --> 00:02:15,030
This is because of poor infrastructure and heavy traffic conditions.
这是因为落后的基础设施和严重的交通拥堵。

20
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:24,490
In Rwanda, those who attend to deadlines with rigid timekeeping are said to be ‘like a typical European’.
在卢旺达，那些严格遵守时间期限的人被说成是“像典型的欧洲人”。

21
00:02:24,490 --> 00:02:33,310
This is in a place where it is not unusual to arrive at 11am for a meeting which started at nine.
在这个地方，上午九点开始的会议，十一点才到也不是什么不寻常的事。

22
00:02:33,310 --> 00:02:40,930
On the other hand, German and Japanese employees mentioned a stricter adherence to time.
另一方面，德国和日本的员工提到了更严格地遵守时间。

23
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:47,340
In Japan, it is common to make an effort to arrive with time to spare for an appointment.
在日本，人们通常会尽量提前一会到达约会地点。

24
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:55,200
Those who arrived at the stroke of nine to a meeting starting at nine a.m. would be considered late.
上午九点开始会议，九点整到达的人会被认为迟到了。

25
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:08,290
In Germany, however, if a dinner party were to begin at eight, a person who had arrived five minutes prior, may walk around the block to ensure that they arrive at eight on the dot.
然而，在德国，如果一个晚宴八点开始，一个提前五分钟到达的人可能会在街区周围四处走走，以确保自己八点准时到达。

26
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:15,440
It seems that the answer is subjective, and what is considered acceptable is based on culture.
似乎答案是主观的，什么是可以接受的是基于文化的。

27
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:22,440
That said, if you don’t mind waiting, it might be best to attend your appointments in good time.
话虽如此，如果你不介意等待，最好还是适时去赴约。

28
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:30,200
And if the worst comes to the worst, remember the old English proverb. Better late than never.
如果情况糟糕到了极点，记住那句古老的英语谚语：迟到总比不到好。
